I fixed the high- and medium-severity items from today's visitor log, wrote a proposal to you, and pushed everything. The build and both checking tools pass. I haven't looked at the changed pages in a browser.

**Research direction (needs your decision).** The four visitor passes found no real physics errors in current claims; every number they recomputed checked out apart from rounding slips. The researcher pass made a point I agree with. The framework's own open questions are now answered, and the results an outside group could use are about other people's theories:
- **Wide binaries:** how often stars hide a close companion is the number the dispute turns on. Newton needs a rate of about 0.45–0.5 per star; the current samples imply about 0.3.
- **Open clusters:** with binaries removed, their internal speeds sit near Newton and well below what modified inertia without an external-field effect predicts.
- **Cassini:** the Solar System measurement excludes the whole tanh-log family of interpolating functions.
- **Dwarf galaxies:** Draco and Crater II exclude any theory whose extra gravity levels off below about 14.7× Newton.

I wrote this up as a proposal in the research repo. It asks whether the explorer should pursue these directly, and whether the companion-rate result should go to Chae's group and to Saad & Ting. That would be outward-facing, so it's your call. Gaia DR4 lands on 2 December, 15 days out.

